(Title) Tj
T*
(Baseline Sleep Dysfunction and Response to Trauma-Focused Psychotherapy in Women With) Tj
T*
(Complex PTSD: A Secondary Analysis) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Sleep disturbances occur frequently in post-traumatic stress disorder and may) Tj
T*
(theoretically interfere with therapeutic learning processes during trauma-focused) Tj
T*
(treatment. To explore this relationship, we conducted a secondary analysis of an existing) Tj
T*
(randomized trial comparing two evidence-based psychotherapy approaches in women with) Tj
T*
(chronic PTSD \(n=178\). Participants received either cognitive processing therapy or) Tj
T*
(dialectical behavior therapy tailored for PTSD, with treatment duration up to 15 months in) Tj
T*
(outpatient settings. Sleep was assessed using both subjective instruments \(self-report) Tj
T*
(questionnaires\) and objective actigraphy measurements at baseline. We examined whether) Tj
T*
(sleep quality predicted subsequent PTSD symptom improvement across the full treatment) Tj
T*
(course. Contrary to initial hypotheses, baseline sleep dysfunction--whether measured) Tj
T*
(subjectively or objectively--did not significantly predict the degree of PTSD symptom) Tj
T*
(reduction during either treatment modality. Depressive symptoms did not modify this) Tj
T*
(relationship. These findings suggest that sleep disturbances do not substantially limit) Tj
T*
(the effectiveness of prolonged, structured trauma-focused psychotherapy in women with) Tj
T*
(complex PTSD presentations, and that initiating evidence-based trauma treatment need not) Tj
T*
(be delayed pending sleep normalization.) Tj
